# Who Wired This - Architecture

## Core Runtime Pattern
- Core managers/UI controllers are scene singletons:
  - `GameManager`, `ScoreManager`, `TimerManager`, `InventoryManager`
  - `HUDController`, `MessagePanel`
- Singleton setup pattern:
  - `if (Instance != null && Instance != this) { Destroy(gameObject); return; }`
  - `Instance = this;`
- No cross-scene persistence pattern is currently used (`DontDestroyOnLoad` is not part of this architecture)

## Scene Flow and Display Topology
- Runtime supports scene switching and scene reload:
  - `SceneHotkeySwitcher` maps keybindings to scene names and calls `SceneManager.LoadScene(..., LoadSceneMode.Single)`
  - `HUDController` restart flow reloads active scene by name
- Multi-display and viewport layout are first-class systems:
  - `ActivateDisplays` activates secondary displays and can normalize render resolution
  - `CameraViewportPresetApplier` applies `ViewportPreset` to `Camera.rect` and `targetDisplay`
  - `DualSingleViewportSwitcher` toggles dual/single viewport layouts at runtime

## Interaction Model
- Nearby interactions use interface-driven discovery:
  - `IInteractable.GetPromptText()`
  - `IInteractable.Interact(GameObject interactor)`
- Player interaction scan currently uses `Physics.OverlapSphere` + nearest candidate selection
- Prompt text can include `$INTERACT$` token and is formatted by `PlayerActions`
- UI prompt updates only when candidate changes (`HUDController.SetInteractPrompt(...)`)

## Puzzle and Scoring Contract
- Puzzle orchestration is interface-based through `IPuzzleManager`:
  - `bool IsSolved`, `int Attempts`, `int HintTriggerAttempt`
  - `event Action OnSuccess`, `event Action<int> OnFailure`
  - `int ComputeCurrentScore()`, `bool TryEngage()`
- Shared puzzle presentation controllers live in `Puzzles/Common` and consume `IPuzzleManager`:
  - `EngageButtonController`, `LCDDisplayController`, `ResultLightController`, `PolaritySwitchController`
- Puzzle manager references in common controllers resolve via `Util/PuzzleManagerResolver` (do not duplicate local resolve helpers)
- Scoring session logic is reusable through `PuzzleScoreSession` (used by matrix/floor-color puzzle flow)

## Inventory/Puzzle Item Contract
- Inventory capacity is fixed (`MaxSlots = 3`)
- Puzzle item matching is ID-based via `ItemData.itemId`
- `TestButton` validates socketed `ItemData.itemId` against expected IDs
- Preserve this contract when adding new puzzle items or sockets

## Dimension and Access Model
- Dimension visibility uses layer-based assignment (`DimensionA`/`DimensionB`) through `DimensionVisibilityObject`
- `DimensionVisibilityObject` can auto-collect renderers/colliders and auto-create a placeholder quad when needed
- Player-specific switch permissions are controlled by `AllowedPlayerTag` and applied to `PolaritySwitchController`

## Event Wiring Expectations
- Managers expose events (for example score/timer/zone changes)
- `IPuzzleManager` consumers subscribe in `Start` and unsubscribe in `OnDestroy`
- For long-lived singleton/UI subscribers, add teardown unsubscription when introducing new listeners
- Avoid hidden side effects in event handlers; keep handlers focused on UI/state refresh

## External Folder Integration
- `WhoWiredThis.Player.PlayerActions` depends on `ThirdPersonMixamo.PlayerController` for interact key and interaction trigger state
- `ThirdPersonMixamo` remains a separate namespace/module for movement/camera rig primitives
- `MiniMap/CameraFollowAbove` is a lightweight standalone minimap camera follower (currently global namespace)

## Safe Extension Guidelines
- Prefer extending existing systems (`IInteractable`, manager events, UI refresh APIs) over creating parallel systems
- Keep user-facing strings centralized in the owning component (for example help/about text in `HUDController`)
- Keep scene reference wiring inspector-driven for UI and interactables
- Prefer using existing scene objects instead of creating runtime objects in code
- Use prefabs for repeatable objects with gameplay meaning (interactables, machines, pickups, reusable UI blocks)
- Connect scene objects to scripts via serialized inspector properties (`[SerializeField]`) rather than lookup/creation code
